In this lesson, we'll look at how to plan a statistical investigation from start to finish. Think of it as a recipe with a clear order of steps. First, you pose a question that can actually be answered with data. Something specific, like how long Year 12 students spend on homework, rather than a vague "do students study a lot?" Next, you choose the variables you need to measure, whether they're numerical like hours, or categorical like subject type. Then you decide on your population, who's in the group you're studying, and pick a sampling method so you can get a fair, manageable slice of that group. After that, you plan the collection carefully. Who will gather the data, what exactly will they record, when, and how. Then you actually collect the data, honestly and without bias. Once you have it, you analyse using suitable graphs and summary statistics, and finally you interpret your findings back in the context of your original question and communicate any limitations. Now let's see it in action.